import pandas as pd
import glob

# ============================================================
# ML-03 — Frame Your Lane as an ML Task
# Lane: E-commerce Product Recommendations / Ranking
# ============================================================

print("=" * 60)
print("1. MY LANE AS AN ML TASK")
print("=" * 60)

print("""
My lane is E-commerce Product Recommendations / Ranking.

Task type: Ranking.

The goal is to rank products for a user's search so that the most
relevant products appear higher in the search results. This is a
ranking problem because the output is an ordered list of products,
rather than only a yes/no prediction.

The ranking supports the decision of which products should appear
first on the search results page.
""")


print("=" * 60)
print("2. TARGET OR PROXY")
print("=" * 60)

print("""
I would predict observed user-product interaction as a proxy for
product relevance.

The label comes from historical observed behavior, such as whether
a user interacted with a product after a search.

This is only a proxy for relevance because an interaction does not
prove that the user purchased the product.
""")


print("=" * 60)
print("3. SUCCESS METRIC")
print("=" * 60)

print("""
Success metric: NDCG (Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain).

NDCG measures whether more relevant products are placed near the
top of the ranking.

A higher NDCG means the ranking is better at putting relevant
products in useful positions near the top of the search results.
""")


print("=" * 60)
print("4. THE UNIT OF ANALYSIS — REAL DATAFRAME")
print("=" * 60)

# Find CSV files
csv_files = glob.glob("**/*.csv", recursive=True)

print("CSV files found:")
for file in csv_files:
    print(" -", file)

# Load the first CSV found
if csv_files:
    data_path = csv_files[0]
    df = pd.read_csv(data_path)

    print("\nDataset:", data_path)
    print("Rows:", len(df))
    print("Columns:", len(df.columns))

    print("\nColumn names:")
    print(df.columns.tolist())

    print("\nFirst 10 rows:")
    display(df.head(10))

    print("""
Unit of analysis:

The dataframe above shows the actual starter data. I will use the
columns and rows shown above to define exactly what one row
represents in this ranking task.
""")

else:
    print("""
No CSV file was found in the repository.
Please check the starter-data location.
""")


print("=" * 60)
print("5. WHY ML BEATS A FIXED RULE")
print("=" * 60)

print("""
A simple fixed rule might rank products using only one factor,
such as the number of previous clicks.

However, product relevance can depend on multiple factors and
their combinations. With many users, searches, and products,
writing a separate if-statement for every situation would be
difficult and inflexible.

Machine learning can learn patterns from historical data and
combine multiple signals to produce a more flexible ranking.

I would still compare the ML approach with a simple baseline rule
to check whether ML actually improves ranking quality.
""")


print("=" * 60)
print("SELF-CHECK")
print("=" * 60)

